{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>And he died, him too.</b> The word גם teaches that both of them died for the same transgression. And our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: granted, Onan — since his father said to him, ״and raise up seed for your brother,״ he reasoned in his mind that the children born would not be called his seed; therefore he שחת ארצה (destroyed [his seed] to the ground). But Er — what reason did he do so? In order that she not conceive and lose her beauty. And Judah's intent when he said, ״and raise up seed for your brother,״ was 'for your brother who died,' in the manner of (Genesis 20): ״say of me, he is my brother,״ which means 'for me.' And from this verse our Rabbis of blessed memory learned: whoever emits seed for naught is liable to death. And this השחתה (corruption) of which it says ״and he destroyed [it] to the ground״ is the sin of the generation of the Flood, of which it is written (Genesis 6): ״for all flesh had corrupted (השחית) its way.״ And Er was called רע (evil) in the eyes of YHWH, and likewise of Onan: ״and it was evil (וירע) in the eyes of YHWH,״ and likewise of the men of Sodom (Genesis 13:12–13): ״and the men of Sodom were evil (רעים) and sinners before YHWH.״"], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 38, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:18:34.965097313Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}